Let q >= 1 be fixed and d=2^q. The target V is any computable matrix in U(d), with certified entry enclosures available to a requested precision. The logical alphabet consists of arbitrary one-qubit U(2) gates and adjacent-qubit CZ on the old logical line. All phases are literal, including global phases for vector/isometry claims. A finite mathematical factorization may use noncomputable choices at exact zero tests; the separate finite-search construction below is the algorithmic claim and does not make such tests.

The physical implementation remains the previously proposed degree-three comb with noninteracting old K_S=E0+sum_i Delta_i n_i, positive computable fixed gaps, and private matched-gap reservoir branches. Within each branch the previously qualified equal-gap one-qubit compiler supplies full-space control, actual full SWAP and clean auxiliary return. Cross-branch gates use only the diagonal old-site CZ. No physical unequal-gap SWAP is introduced. All readiness, independent vacuum for preparation, microscopic control, phase reference, timing, reserve, calibration, initial/final energy allowances and finite-energy parent premises remain supplied. A separate exposed review has identified the static-field accounting correction: a driven-control bound g/2 is not an added-static-plus-control bound; the latter needs Delta_max+g/2. This new proposal adopts that correction explicitly.

## 2. Controlled one-qubit SU(2), with exact phases

Products act right to left. Write E_X(theta)=exp(i theta X), C=CZ(c,t), and let b be the requested value of the control bit. Then

  E_X(theta/2) C E_X((-1)^b theta/2) C

acts as E_X(theta) when c=b and as identity when c!=b. Indeed on c=0 the two exponents add, and on c=1 conjugation by Z changes the second exponent's sign. This is an exact four-letter word (two one-qubit rotations and two CZ gates), for either positive or negative control. No extraneous phase is discarded.

With S=diag(1,i), S X S*=Y, so controlled E_Y(theta) costs six letters by conjugating the controlled-X word by S and S*. Every SU(2) matrix has an X-Y-X Euler representation, so a singly controlled SU(2) gate costs at most 4+6+4=14 letters. This applies to either control polarity. A gate with no controls is one arbitrary one-qubit SU(2) letter.

For k>=2 controls, split the controls into disjoint nonempty groups of sizes floor(k/2) and ceil(k/2). Every U in SU(2) is a commutator A B A* B* in SU(2): write U=exp(i theta v.sigma), choose B=exp(-i theta v.sigma/2), and choose A=i w.sigma with unit w perpendicular to v. Then A B A*=B*, and the commutator is exp(i theta v.sigma). At U=+/-I any suitable perpendicular pair is allowed. Use the first control group to condition A,A*, and the second to condition B,B*. If either group fails, its controlled factors become identities and the remaining pair cancels. If both pass, the required U acts. Mixed control polarities cause no additional cost.

Define T(1)=1 and T(k)=2T(floor(k/2))+2T(ceil(k/2)). Thus k-controlled SU(2) costs at most 14T(k), and the already used recurrence estimate T(k)<=9k^2/8 gives a convenient polynomial bound. No control qubit is changed by this construction, including on failed patterns.

## 3. A two-level SU(2) on arbitrary logical basis states

For two distinct bit strings b,b' choose a pivot p where they differ, relabeling the pair so b_p=1 and b'_p=0. For each other mismatch j apply CNOT(p,j). The resulting permutation K fixes b' and maps b to a string differing from b' only at p. Each CNOT is H_j CZ(p,j) H_j, exactly three logical letters. Apply the (q-1)-controlled SU(2) on p conditioned on the common spectator pattern, conjugated by K and K*. Choose the local matrix ordering consistently with the ordered pair b,b'. The resulting operation is the requested SU(2) on that pair and identity on every other basis state; K has no permutation phases. If the Hamming distance is t, the cost is at most

  14T(q-1)+6(t-1) <= 14T(q-1)+6(q-1),       q>=2.

Logical CNOT and K may exchange old excitation labels during the logical computation. Their physical realizations are compositions of separately conserving branch gates and diagonal CZ on encoded states. They are not physical swaps between unequal local Hamiltonians.

## 4. The determinant phase is part of the program

Complex SU(2) Givens elimination uses at most p=d(d-1)/2 two-level matrices to reduce V to D=diag(1,...,1,det V). Specifically, on a pair (a,b), when r=sqrt(|a|^2+|b|^2)>0, [[conj(a)/r,conj(b)/r],[-b/r,a/r]] has determinant one and sends (a,b) to (r,0). If r=0 use identity for the mathematical existence argument. Unitarity then makes each completed pivot +1, leaving only det V on the last basis state. Reverse the elimination with adjoints and include D explicitly.

For any bit string b and P_b=|b><b|,

  P_b = 2^-q sum_{J subset {1,...,q}} (-1)^(sum_{j in J} b_j) Z_J.

The terms commute. To implement exp(i theta P_b), implement its empty-subset scalar exp(i theta/2^q) by a one-qubit scalar unitary exp(i theta/2^q) I, plus the nonempty Z-string rotations. For a nonempty J, select an anchor t in J and conjugate its one-qubit Z rotation by CNOT(j,t) for j in J\{t}. This maps Z_t to product_{j in J}Z_j. Each such conjugation costs 2(|J|-1) CNOT gates, or 6(|J|-1)+1 logical letters including the rotation. Thus the full phase word costs at most

  1 + sum_{nonempty J} [6(|J|-1)+1]
    = (3q-5)2^q + 6.

This includes the scalar; omission is not allowed. For q>=2 a complete-graph logical word for every V therefore has length at most

  R_full(q)=p[14T(q-1)+6(q-1)]+(3q-5)2^q+6.

For q=1 take R_full(1)=1 and use V itself as one U(2) letter.

## 5. Reduce logical connectivity to the old line

For q>=2 an arbitrary CZ between two old logical sites can be realized by moving one logical operand along the old line to a neighbor of the other, applying adjacent CZ, and reversing every move. At most q-2 logical SWAPs occur in each direction. An adjacent logical SWAP is exactly three CNOT gates, each H-CZ-H, so costs nine adjacent-alphabet letters. All other logical wires return to their original positions. Thus a routed CZ costs at most 18(q-2)+1 letters. Arbitrary one-qubit letters already have the permitted support. The deliberately loose common bound

  R(q)=(18q+1)R_full(q), q>=2;       R(1)=1

therefore bounds all words using only arbitrary one-qubit U(2) and adjacent CZ, independent of V and accuracy. Any implementation of the logical SWAP uses the same branch and diagonal controls as the other logical words; this step grants no new physical SWAP permission.

## 6. A finite-error algorithm without exact zero decisions

The exact factorization establishes existence and a uniform finite bound. For algorithmic synthesis, enumerate all words of length <=R(q) over the finite choices of one-qubit sites and adjacent CZ edges, and all one-qubit U(2) grid gates. A concrete U(2) parametrization is exp(i alpha) exp(i beta X) exp(i gamma Y) exp(i delta X); compact angle ranges cover U(2). Each angle can be rounded on a finite periodic grid with absolute wrapped error <=h. The operator-norm error of a rounded one-qubit letter is <=4h by telescoping and ||exp(i a P)-exp(i b P)||<=|a-b| for P=I,X,Y. Consequently every exact word of length <=R(q) has a grid word within 4R(q)h of it.

Given eta_program>0 choose a rational grid width bound h<eta_program/[16 max(1,R(q))]. The existence argument guarantees at least one candidate with error <eta_program/4. For each finite candidate, compute convergent certified enclosures for its entries and for V. The Frobenius norm upper bound is enough: ||A||<=||A||_F<=sqrt(d)||A||. Choose the grid still finer, for example h<eta_program/[16 sqrt(d) max(1,R(q))], so some candidate has Frobenius error <eta_program/4. Refine entry enclosures and accept only a certified strict inequality ||V_grid-V||_F<eta_program. Dovetail precision refinement across the finite candidate set so a boundary candidate cannot stall the search. A strict successful margin exists, hence termination follows. This is a finite computable program, with all global phases tested; no polynomial classical-time guarantee is made. A target must really be a computable unitary with certified enclosures; an arbitrary unsupported floating matrix does not satisfy that premise.

The word length may vary with target/precision, but it is always bounded by R(q). Subsequent physical compiler error is a distinct budget eta_compile, allocated across at most R(q) branch letters. A logical program approximation error must not be silently spent a second time as physical calibration error.

